# Line-ending policy: LF everywhere, for every platform.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites LF to CRLF in the
# working tree. Then the repository's line endings depend on who committed last, and
# an LF-only source file can arrive on someone else's machine as CRLF. This file plus
# `core.autocrlf=false` pin it: what is committed is what everyone gets.
#
# The PowerShell scripts are LF too, deliberately. PowerShell 5.1 runs LF scripts
# fine — the AST parser and the scripts themselves were both exercised on LF-only
# files. Only `*.ps1` was ever at risk of looking "wrong" in Notepad, which is not a
# reason to make the whole repository platform-dependent.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Genuinely binary files: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary

# Lockfiles are generated and large; keep them out of diffs.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
